  await d.list({ title: '6.1 · The Five Commitments', fontSize: 26, rows: [
    { icon: 'FaBrain', label: 'C1', text: 'I Know My Biases' },
    { icon: 'FaThermometerHalf', label: 'C2', text: 'I Manage My Emotional Impact' },
    { icon: 'FaShieldAlt', label: 'C3', text: 'I Build Psychological Safety Deliberately' },
    { icon: 'FaCogs', label: 'C4', text: 'I Use Process to Protect Quality' },
    { icon: 'FaUsers', label: 'C5', text: 'I Treat People as the Strategy' }],
    notes: N(
      '6.1 · THE FIVE COMMITMENTS: SELF-SCORING ASSESSMENT\nTime: 15–18 minutes including scoring and debrief (this slide and the next).',
      'Setting up the assessment. Before participants begin scoring, read the instruction aloud: “Score what is consistently true of your practice right now. The value of this tool is entirely dependent on your honesty.” Allow silence to settle before they begin.',
      'Participant activity (participant file, Section 6.1): individually and in silence, participants work through each commitment tab (C1 through C5) and score themselves 1–5 on each statement, then click Results. Allow 10–12 minutes for completion without interruption.\nScale: 1 = Rarely/never · 2 = Occasionally · 3 = Sometimes · 4 = Often · 5 = Consistently. Twenty statements, four per commitment; each commitment totals out of 20; overall score out of 100.',
      'C1 · I Know My Biases — I actively work to identify the patterns in my thinking that may not serve the organisation’s interests — particularly in the areas of overconfidence, confirmation, and status quo bias.\n1. When I receive information that confirms my existing view of a situation, I actively look for what it might be missing, overstating, or leaving out.\n2. I can name two or three specific patterns in my own thinking that have led me toward a poor call or a missed signal — and I actively watch for them.\n3. When something I championed does not go as expected, I examine what in my reasoning contributed to it.\n4. I regularly and deliberately seek out perspectives that challenge how I am reading a situation, particularly from people who are likely to see it differently from me.',
      'C2 · I Manage My Emotional Impact — I recognise that my emotional state — visible or not — sets the climate for how those around me think, speak up, and engage. I take responsibility for that climate.\n1. I am aware, in real time, of how my visible enthusiasm, scepticism, or frustration shapes what others feel able to say or contribute in the room.\n2. When I feel strongly that a particular direction or course of action is right, I consciously moderate how I signal that — to preserve the space for others to form and express their own views.\n3. I notice when those around me appear to be calibrating what they say to match what they think I want to hear, and I take deliberate steps to open that space back up.\n4. I can point to a recent moment where I visibly reconsidered my position based on input from someone with less authority or experience than me.',
      'C3 · I Build Psychological Safety Deliberately — I create the conditions in which honest, constructive challenge is expected and protected. I am the first to be challenged and the last to shut challenge down.\n1. The people I lead know — from observable evidence — that they can push back on a view I have taken publicly without personal or professional risk.\n2. In conversations I lead, I actively and specifically invite challenge and dissenting views before anyone volunteers them.\n3. When someone challenges my thinking, my first visible response is to engage with what they have raised.\n4. I recognise and acknowledge intellectual honesty — including views that are inconvenient or that cut against the prevailing direction — at least as visibly as I recognise agreement.',
      'C4 · I Use Process to Protect Quality — I do not rely on my own judgement alone. I use structured approaches to counteract the predictable failures of unassisted human reasoning.\n1. Before committing to a significant course of action, I use at least one deliberate, structured approach to pressure-test my thinking alongside instinct and experience.\n2. I actively look for relevant precedents or comparable situations that can help calibrate my assumptions before I commit to a direction.\n3. I separate the work of opening up possibilities from the work of narrowing down and committing — to reduce the risk of settling prematurely on a preferred path.\n4. I can recall a specific moment where a structured or deliberately designed thinking process led me to a different conclusion than my initial instinct would have.',
      'C5 · I Treat People as the Strategy — I invest in the human quality of my team’s thinking as well as in frameworks and data. I know that the best process produces the best outcome.\n1. I invest deliberate attention in how the people I lead think and engage with each other, as well as in the quality of what they produce.\n2. I design conversations and working sessions to draw out the best available thinking in the room before a direction forms.\n3. I treat the diversity of experience, background, and perspective in my team as something that actively improves the quality of what we arrive at together.\n4. I can point to evidence that the way my team thinks and works together produces better outcomes than individual expertise or data alone would.') });
  await d.list({ title: '6.1 · Score interpretation', fontSize: 26, rows: [
    { icon: 'FaTrophy', label: '85–100', text: 'Leading Practice' },
    { icon: 'FaCheckCircle', label: '65–84', text: 'Established Awareness' },
    { icon: 'FaSeedling', label: '45–64', text: 'Developing Awareness' },
    { icon: 'FaSun', label: '25–44', text: 'Emerging Awareness' },
    { icon: 'FaExclamationTriangle', gold: true, label: '20–24', text: 'At Risk' }],
    notes: N(
      '6.1 · VIEWING RESULTS AND DEBRIEF',
      'After the assessment: participants open their Results and read the interpretation for their level before any discussion begins.',
      'Watch for (facilitation caution): Do not ask participants to share their overall score publicly. Invite them to share only what they are comfortable sharing. The assessment is a personal diagnostic. Reinforce this explicitly before the debrief.',
      'Group debrief — three questions:\n1. “What did your score reveal that surprised you?”\n2. “Which single commitment had the lowest sub-score — and what does that tell you?”\n3. “What is one concrete action you will take before your next strategy session based on what this assessment surfaced?”',
      'Score interpretation:\n85–100 · Leading Practice: Your self-assessment suggests a high and consistent level of behavioural awareness in your leadership practice. You are operating with genuine insight into your own thinking patterns, creating the conditions for honest challenge, and protecting the quality of your judgement through deliberate process. At this level the priority shifts from building the practice to sustaining it under pressure — and extending it to the people and environments around you. Consider whether what you have built depends on your presence, or whether it is becoming embedded in how those you lead think and work.\n65–84 · Established Awareness: You have developed meaningful behavioural competence and it is visible in your day-to-day leadership. There are areas of genuine strength alongside areas where consistency may vary — particularly when conditions are pressured, ambiguous, or moving fast. Review your per-commitment scores to identify where your practice holds most reliably and where it is most dependent on circumstances being favourable. The transition from established to leading typically requires active focus on the commitments that feel least natural.\n45–64 · Developing Awareness: You have clear awareness of these commitments and an emerging practice — but gaps exist between what you know and what you do with consistency. This is a common and productive stage: the understanding is in place; the habits are still forming. Focus initially on one or two commitments where the gap between aspiration and practice feels smallest, and build from there. Behavioural leadership literacy develops through repeated, intentional application in real moments where the stakes are present.\n25–44 · Emerging Awareness: Your score suggests that while some awareness of these commitments exists, systematic practice is not yet in place across most of them. Leaders at this level often understand what good looks like but have not yet created the conditions — in their own habits, their team culture, or their working processes — that translate awareness into discipline. Identify the commitment where your score is lowest: this is likely where your leadership is most exposed and where focused attention will have the greatest impact.\n20–24 · At Risk: A score at this level suggests that the quality of thinking and judgement in your leadership environment may be significantly exposed to unmanaged cognitive and social bias. This signals that the practices, conditions, and structures required to protect that quality are not yet in place. Treat this as a priority finding. The costs of unmanaged bias in leadership — in poor calls, missed signals, and eroded trust — tend to accumulate gradually in ways that are difficult to trace until they become expensive to reverse.',
      'A final note. Self-assessment is the beginning of the work. The most useful thing you can do with this tool is to share what it surfaced with someone who observes you closely, and to let that become a real conversation.') });
