  await d.prompt({ icon: 'FaRedo', label: 'Unit Summary · Return to the opening question', text: 'What assumption did you hold about how good decisions get made in your organisation? Has anything shifted?',
    foot: 'The one thing I am taking from this unit that I intend to act on is…',
    notes: N(
      'UNIT SUMMARY · UNIT 1 SYNTHESIS',
      'Closing the unit: Read the summary aloud or ask a participant to read it. The summary is intentionally compact — it is designed to crystallise the arc of the unit in under two minutes. Do not add to it. Let it do its work.',
      'Unit Summary (facilitator file):\nUnit 1: Behavioural Strategy Fundamentals establishes the psychological bedrock that underpins all strategic decision-making.\nIt opens by defining behavioural strategy as the intersection of cognitive psychology and strategic management — making the case that strategy is a deeply human exercise shaped by invisible mental filters. The unit introduces Kahneman’s dual-process framework (System 1 fast thinking vs. System 2 deliberate thinking) as the core framework through which leaders should understand their own judgement.\nFrom there, it maps 20 cognitive biases across four families — Action-Oriented, Stability, Interest, and Social — showing how each distorts strategic decisions in predictable, measurable ways, and what the organisational warning signs look like in practice.\nThe unit then traces where these biases show up across the four phases of any strategy process: Diagnosis, Option Generation, Decision, and Execution — demonstrating that bias is a process vulnerability. It adds a second dimension through the emotional climate, showing how fear, ego, or grounded confidence in a leadership environment either amplifies or dampens the quality of thinking.\nIt closes with practical intervention — six debiasing principles, three pillars for building collective intelligence, and institutional practices for embedding behavioural discipline — culminating in a simulation exercise and a self-scoring leadership assessment across five commitments.\nThe central argument of the unit, in one line: the quality of your strategy is a direct function of the quality of the thinking environment in which it was made.',
      'Return to the opening question: “What assumption did you hold about how good decisions get made in your organisation?” Ask 2–3 participants: “Has anything shifted — and if so, what specifically?”',
      'Closing the room: Ask each participant to complete this sentence privately in writing: “The one thing I am taking from this unit that I intend to act on is…” Invite 2–3 to share voluntarily. This is the unit’s final anchoring moment.',
      'Transition to Module 2: Direct participants to the “Unit 1 Complete” button. Remind them that Module 2 — Direction — builds directly on the foundation established here. The behavioural awareness developed in Unit 1 is the filter through which all subsequent strategy work should pass.') });

